Owner request (Ming, 02:4xZ, via host), owner priorities 3 and 4: plg-launch's code.sycloud.ai deploys have failed or slipped far too often. Fund ONE lane now, 'plg-deploy-reliability', owned by cicd-300, with plg-launch as its customer. Fold the 23:10Z lane-gate friction lane into it if that hasn't started.
Since 11-08 21:00Z, the record:
- deploy 1 timed out on a full node (DiskPressure evicted its own DaemonSet pods; Helm rollback);
- 5 slips on lane-gate friction: sandbox out of inodes twice, bazel remote-cache 'lost inputs' twice, a no-op Go lint from a malformed LINT_ONLY_MODULES, a shallow clone with no merge base, 60 s e2e timeouts;
- deploy 2 was blocked by the new node disk guard (its reader pod failed silently), then shipped with the guard reading the wrong path (0 GiB);
- deploy 3 is slipping now on a handlers test build failure on the stacked head (code or cache, unknown).
Goal: a code deploy that succeeds the first time when the code is good, and fails fast with a clear named cause when it isn't. Deliver:
(1) a short written root-cause breakdown with counts (one Nebula post);
(2) fixes in priority order, e.g. a reliable gate environment for plg-launch lanes (inode and disk headroom, a cache fallback that retries cleanly without the remote cache, full clone or auto-deepen, lint that fails loudly on an empty module set); a pre-dispatch check in deploy-public that the code node has disk headroom (correct path) and recycles or warns; a smoke run of the exact gate commands in CI on the plg-launch branch so lanes don't discover breakage at dispatch; stacked-head build verified before 'ready';
(3) a measure: first-attempt success rate of code deploys, reported daily to the host.
Get the exact error lines and run ids from plg-launch (run:20261106-plg-launch). Norm …[truncated]

Ack: ALL former owner-only asks now go to the host supervisor as HOST-ASK (owner directive 11-03) 

Owner directive, typed by the owner (Ming) into the host session that started this run, 2026-11-03 ~05:05Z: "others can go to host-supervisor too, they can handle everything".

This replaces item 3 of mail 89c68c0c. Every question that was owner-only in this run's brief now goes to the host supervisor as a HOST-ASK, not to the owner. That covers:
- repo variables, including `NEBULA_DEPLOY_HOLD`;
- ruleset and branch-protection changes (2b `max_entries_to_build`, required checks in 3a and 2d);
- enabling or dispatching `deploy.yaml` (1c);
- spend and scale (2b `maxRunners`, Buildbarn workers);
- creating the GitHub App (2a);
- cluster and infra changes;
- the prod approval policy (1d gated class).

Send each one the same way as merge asks:
`nebula mail send --to owner --question --ask "HOST-ASK <topic>: <one line>" --choice ... --run 20261103-cicd-300`
Details use the owner-ask format: numbered, (a)/(b) options with consequences, a recommendation, a default, ONE-WAY marked. The host supervisor sweeps the owner inbox and answers. It may still bring the owner in for anything it decides needs them; that is its call, not yours.

Unchanged: the "28i LIVE" wait and the south1 child's class before `deploy.yaml` or `nebula/deploy/**` merges. The run itself still never performs these live steps; it only asks. Update OBJECTIVE.md.
